# VisionInspect AI

An animated, industrial-style dashboard for inspecting parts with an ESP32-CAM,
plus a small Python/OpenCV backend that does the actual computer-vision work.

## Files

- `index.html`, `styles.css`, `script.js` — the website (static, deployable to GitHub Pages)
- `app.py`, `requirements.txt` — optional local backend that talks to the ESP32-CAM and runs defect detection
- `history.json`, `calibration.json` — created automatically by `app.py` to persist data

## 1. Run the website

You can just open `index.html` in a browser, or host the three frontend files
on GitHub Pages (Settings → Pages → deploy from the branch containing these
files). No build step, no Node server needed.

## 2. Connect the ESP32-CAM

On the Dashboard, enter your ESP32-CAM's IP (e.g. `192.168.1.10`), the port
(usually `80`) and endpoint (usually `/capture`), then click **Connect
Camera**. The site polls that URL to show a live feed and measure latency.

Your computer/phone must be on the **same Wi-Fi network** as the ESP32-CAM.

## 3. Run the AI backend (for real defect detection)

The website can show a **demo/simulated** result on its own so you can present
it without any setup. For real analysis of the camera image, run the backend:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python app.py
```

This starts a local API on `http://localhost:5000`. In the website's
**Settings → AI Backend URL**, keep it pointed at `http://localhost:5000`
(or wherever you deploy `app.py`). The backend fetches frames directly from
the ESP32-CAM, runs the OpenCV analysis (adapted from the original detection
script), and returns a JSON result that the site renders, speaks, and logs.

### API

| Method | Endpoint | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/status` | backend + calibration status |
| GET | `/api/frame` | proxy a single frame from the ESP32-CAM |
| POST | `/api/connect` | test connectivity + latency |
| POST | `/api/inspect` | capture + analyze a frame, returns result JSON |
| GET | `/api/history` | inspection records the backend has saved |
| POST | `/api/calibrate` | store a reference mm/px pair for measurement |

## 4. Calibration

Millimeter measurements are **visual estimates**, not calibrated metrology.
In Settings, place a known reference object in frame, measure its width in
pixels (e.g. from a captured image) and enter both values under
**Calibration** to get a pixels-per-mm conversion.

## Notes

- The frontend keeps its own history/settings in the browser's local storage,
  so it works standalone even without the backend running.
- When the backend is reachable, inspections use real OpenCV analysis instead
  of the demo simulator.
- Voice announcements use the browser's built-in Speech Synthesis — no API
  key or external service required.
